[Net test: route search always clears the previous search's marks]
author=reverse engineering test patch; executed in the function-level interpreter on ten levels; not run on a live game
description=Hardening for a rare corner case. Before each route search the game wipes the marks the previous search left on the path grid - except when that previous search started on the very first node of the grid, which it mistakes for "no previous search". Your machine's prediction of your own character can make route searches the other machines do not, so in that one case leftover marks could send a monster a different way on your machine. This removes the exception (path_service, 0x184A08). Only five levels have walkable floor on that corner node, and the arena is not one of them. FINDINGS V43, V45. Every player must use the same setting.
// was: 10400060 (beqz v0,+0x60)
patch=1,EE,00184A08,word,00000000

[Net test: menu screens do not recompute player statistics]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V47); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=The stats page, the skills page and the item list each recompute every player's statistics while they are drawn, on the machine that has the menu open only. That recompute also trims hit points and mana to their maximum, so after a heal that briefly overshoots, the machine with a menu open trims early and the others do not: hit points differ, and later a death happens on one machine only. In a five-million-tick soak this was the one cause left, in 7 of 27 pairs that had a healing skill. This removes the three drawing-side calls (0x1D2E7C, 0x1D5950, 0x1CE530); the simulation's own recompute, which every machine runs at the same moment, is untouched. A menu left open may show slightly stale numbers until it is closed. FINDINGS K4, V46. Each machine fixes itself; use on every machine.
// was: 0C06D916 (jal player_recalcStats) at all three addresses
patch=1,EE,001D2E7C,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,001D5950,word,00000000
patch=1,EE,001CE530,word,00000000

[Net test: stats page does not recompute the main weapon's damage either]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V47); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=Companion to "stats page does not rewrite stored weapon damage", which removed the off-hand recompute. The page's other recompute, for the main weapon (0x1D2C38), still writes the stored attack rating on the owner's machine only - seen in the soak after changing equipment with the menu open. This removes it as well. The stats page then shows the attack rating and damage from before the equipment change until the menu is closed. FINDINGS K3, V46. Each machine fixes itself; use on every machine.
// was: 0C06DC56 (jal Player_recalcDamage)
patch=1,EE,001D2C38,word,00000000

[Net test: carried weight is recomputed only inside the simulation]
author=reverse engineering test patch; confirmed on two linked Play! emulator instances (FINDINGS V57: the pair that diverged agrees with it, and seven pairs that differed in this figure show no difference in 248,654 ticks); untested on PCSX2 or a console
description=A character's carried weight is stored in its record and decides whether an item can be picked up. Some changes to the bags (a recall potion, a shop purchase into a new slot, items removed by a script or a skill) do not update it - but the owner's machine updates it anyway, outside the simulation, through its prediction of its own character and through four menu screens. The owner's machine can then allow or refuse a pick-up that the other machines decide differently, and the game goes out of sync. This makes the predicted character skip that update (0x1A9578, with a nine-word routine in the unused function at 0x257B0C, after the words other sections use) and removes the four menu-side updates. The weight shown in a menu can lag until the simulation next updates it, which also makes the shop's "too heavy to buy" test looser. FINDINGS V57. Every player should use the same setting.
